[by Claude Code] Fixed in b239f2b516. `_rust_fallback_reason` now calls the loader's new `probe_rust_toolchain`, the helper `_build_context` also uses, so the TreeCore check takes the same lock (`toolchain_lock_path()`, the default extension cache's `locks/toolchain.lock`) and runs the same commands in the same cwd. It keeps its 10 s bound, which now also covers the wait for the lock, so a scheduler queued behind another process's probe still falls back to the Python TreeCore within 10 s.

`test_concurrent_schedulers_probe_the_toolchain_one_at_a_time` runs `select_tree_core_backend` in four processes against stand-in `cargo`/`rustc` scripts that record overlapping queries. It fails with the previous unlocked queries and passes with this change.

@chrikrah this also takes your earlier suggestion: one deadline now covers the lock wait and both queries, and the error says "the Rust toolchain probe timed out after 300 s waiting for ...". `test_toolchain_probe_timeout_includes_the_wait_for_the_lock` holds the lock in another process and checks that a 0.5 s probe gives up without running a query; it fails when the lock wait ignores the deadline.

